<body>
<div id="tip" role="tooltip"></div>
<main class="wrap">

<header style="display:grid;gap:10px">
  <span class="eyebrow">slop-code-bench · Parnas pilot · seed 1 · 2026-11-09 to 11-11</span>
  <h1>Does the design instruction change what the agent builds?</h1>
  <div class="panel" style="display:grid;gap:8px">
    <p style="margin:0"><b>Goal.</b> Using SlopCodeBench tasks, test whether a design instruction given only at the first checkpoint makes later feature additions cheaper and more correct. At each later checkpoint a fresh agent session, with no memory of the instruction, adds the next feature to the code it finds.</p>
    <p style="margin:0"><b>Questions.</b> (1) Does the instruction change the structure of the code the agent builds? (2) If it does, do later checkpoints pass more tests, break fewer earlier tests, or cost less?</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin:0">This is a pilot: one run per condition per task, to see whether a difference shows up at all before running more seeds.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="lede">Four long, multi-checkpoint coding tasks, each run once under three prompt conditions. Same model (opus-5.5), same harness, same hidden tests. c1 adds nothing to the first-checkpoint prompt; c2 adds one sentence and c3 a five-sentence design instruction.</p>
  <p class="small muted">Tasks, specs and hidden tests come from SlopCodeBench (SCBench): <a href="https://www.scbench.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scbench.ai</a></p>
  <div class="legend" id="legendTop"></div>
</header>

<div class="caveats">
  <strong>Read these before comparing</strong>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Dollar cost is list price, not what was paid.</b> Tokens are priced at Claude Opus 5.5 API rates: input $4, output $20, 1-hour cache write $8 and cache read $0.20 per million tokens. Every cache write in every trace is a 1-hour write, all calls ran on claude-opus-5-5 with global routing and standard speed, and the result matches the harness's own cost to the cent in 59 of 60 checkpoints (sith c1 checkpoint 2: $6.10 here vs $6.07). The runs used a subscription login.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section id="method">
  <h2>How the runs were done</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="display:grid;gap:8px">
  <p style="margin:0"><b>Why a fresh start.</b> To isolate the effect of code structure, each checkpoint started in a fresh session with only the code carried over; agent-written notes and documents were removed. This is stricter than normal use, where notes persist.</p>
  <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:20px;display:grid;gap:6px">
    <li><b>Harness.</b> The SlopCodeBench harness, driving Claude Code (v2.1.310) with Claude Opus 5.5, thinking set to high, a 300-step cap per checkpoint, and permissions bypassed inside the container.</li>
    <li><b>Docker.</b> The agent worked in a Python 3.12 container on an internal network. Its only way out was a proxy that allowed the Anthropic API and PyPI. Web search and web fetch were disabled, no MCP servers were loaded, and non-essential traffic was turned off.</li>
    <li><b>Only checkpoint 1 differs.</b> The design instruction (none, modular, or information hiding) appears only in the first prompt. Every later checkpoint gets the same text in all three conditions: the new spec plus "keep using the same virtual environment". So checkpoints 2 onward are built on whatever the agent made at checkpoint 1.</li>
    <li><b>Fresh start at each checkpoint, own code kept.</b> Before each new checkpoint the harness threw away the container and started a new one, and cleared Claude's home directory (no conversation, memory or history carried over). The workspace (the agent's code, its virtual environment and requirements.txt) was kept. Markdown files the agent wrote (for example c3's DESIGN.md in recli) were deleted, so the agent could rely only on the code itself.</li>
    <li><b>Hidden tests.</b> After each checkpoint the benchmark's tests ran in a separate environment against a snapshot of the code. The agent never saw the tests or the results, and the tests were not changed. Tests from earlier checkpoints run again at every later checkpoint, so one early miss is counted several times. The page calls each of these counts a <b>failed test instance</b>: a test that fails at 4 checkpoints counts as 4 instances.</li>
    <li><b>Checks per cell.</b> Every checkpoint was checked for an identical set of 17 built-in skills, no account skills, no MCP, zero skill or MCP calls, and no mention of the test or solution folders in the trace. All 12 cells passed.</li>
  </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="conditions">
  <h2>The three conditions</h2>
  <p class="small muted">Each first-checkpoint prompt is the benchmark's default text. c2 adds one sentence and c3 a five-sentence design instruction. Every later checkpoint gets the same continuation text in every condition, so the conditions differ only at the start.</p>
  <div class="grid3" id="condCards"></div>
  <div class="panel" style="display:grid;gap:8px">
    <h3>Checkpoints 2 onward: identical for every condition</h3>
    <div class="prompt" id="contPrompt"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="tasks">
  <h2>The four tasks</h2>
  <div class="grid4" id="taskCards"></div>
</section>

<section id="whole">
  <h2>Whole task: all checkpoints combined</h2>
  <p class="small muted">Each row is one measure. Each column is one task, with its own scale starting at zero. Within a cell, bars compare c1, c2 and c3. The bold value is the best of the three: fewer failures, less time, or less code complexity. Hover a bar for the exact value.</p>
  <div class="ov" id="ov"></div>
</section>

<section id="differences">
  <h2>What differs</h2>
  <div class="grid4" id="notes"></div>
</section>

<section id="byck">
  <h2>Checkpoint by checkpoint</h2>
  <div class="tabs" role="tablist" id="tabs"></div>
  <p class="small muted" id="tabNote"></p>
  <div class="ctl"><span class="small muted">Effort, cost and token charts show</span><div class="seg" id="cumSeg"><button type="button" data-cum="0" aria-pressed="true">Per checkpoint</button><button type="button" data-cum="1" aria-pressed="false">Running total</button></div></div>
  <h3>Tests</h3>
  <p class="small muted">Pass rate and failed-test count carry the same information: at each checkpoint, failed = total − passed (minus a few skips), and every condition runs the same tests. Comparing failed tests is easier to read, and the third chart removes the tests that fail in all three conditions, leaving only what separates them.</p>
  <div class="charts" id="chartsTests"></div>
  <h3>Effort</h3>
  <div class="charts" id="chartsEffort"></div>
  <h3>Cost and tokens</h3>
  <div class="charts" id="chartsCost"></div>
  <p class="small muted">Split by kind. Cache-read is 10–100× larger than the others, so it has its own chart; it grows with steps × context size.</p>
  <div class="charts" id="chartsTok"></div>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <div class="charts" id="chartsCode"></div>
  <h3>Per-checkpoint table</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table id="cktable"></table></div>
  <h3>Tests that failed in some conditions but not all</h3>
  <p class="small muted" id="fmNote"></p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="fm" id="fm"></table></div>
  <details class="panel"><summary id="allFailSum"></summary><div class="mono small" id="allFailList" style="margin-top:10px;display:grid;gap:2px"></div></details>
  <h3>Tool calls by tool</h3>
  <div class="tools" id="tools"></div>
</section>

<section id="qualitative">
  <h2>What the agents did: navigation, search, implementation, testing</h2>
  <p class="small muted">Read from the step-by-step traces of all 12 cells. One reviewer per task read the three conditions side by side; a sample of claims was checked against the code and the test logs. One run per condition: these describe what happened in these runs, not effects of the prompts. Full reports: <code>features/scbench/analysis/qual/</code>.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="panel qbox">
      <h3>Same in every condition</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Navigation.</b> Each checkpoint opened with <code>ls</code>/<code>find</code>, then dumping most of the code with <code>cat</code> or <code>sed</code>. With modules (c2, c3) that meant reading nearly every module again; c1 read slices of its one file.</li>
        <li><b>Search.</b> Done through shell commands. No run used the Grep or Glob tools or read its own docs.</li>
        <li><b>Editing.</b> Mostly Python find-and-replace scripts or whole-file rewrites run in the shell, rarely the Edit tool.</li>
        <li><b>Steps before the first edit</b> were similar across conditions, with no consistent ordering.</li>
        <li><b>Finishing.</b> Every checkpoint ended with the agent saying the spec was done, but some hidden tests still failed.</li>
        <li><b>The design instruction</b> was never mentioned again after checkpoint 1.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="panel qbox">
      <h3>Different by condition</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Shape.</b> c1 kept one growing file in every task (sith ~6,700 lines). c2 built a package. c3 built a package whose module docstrings each name a "Secret:".</li>
        <li><b>Spread of edits.</b> c2 and c3 touched many files per feature (recli checkpoints 2–8: 4 / 29 / 34 files edited); c1 re-read and patched its one file.</li>
        <li><b>Hub files.</b> Even in packages one or two files grew every checkpoint (recli c2 <code>upgrade.py</code> 875 lines; c3 <code>catalog.py</code>, and <code>messages.py</code> holding every message).</li>
        <li><b>Own tests.</b> c1 never wrote a test suite. c2 and c3 did in sith and recli (recli ends with 208 and 217 own tests). In eve_industry and metric_transform_lang no condition wrote unit tests.</li>
        <li><b>Module boundaries.</b> In metric_transform_lang c2 called another module's private function; c3 added public helpers instead.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="panel qbox">
      <h3>Struggles and weaknesses</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Failures trace to spec readings, not structure.</b> No failure in any task was traced to duplicated logic or a bypassed interface.</li>
        <li><b>One early miss repeats.</b> A misread at checkpoint 2 is counted again at every later checkpoint, which drives most gaps between conditions.</li>
        <li><b>Own tests confirmed own readings.</b> c2's sith tests asserted the wrong name <code>NoneType</code>; in recli c2 and c3 saw the breaking first-run message in their own tests and changed the tests, not the program.</li>
        <li><b>Explaining evidence away.</b> eve_industry c1 found the ships its relic rule missed and called them "invented from Tech I blueprints".</li>
        <li><b>Abandoned checks.</b> eve_industry c2 tried to test the 100% clamp, the probe broke, and it moved on.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">Techniques: how often each was used</h3>
  <p class="small muted">Counts of tool calls in the step-by-step traces, all checkpoints together. One shell command can count in more than one row (for example an edit script followed by a probe). Highest of the three conditions per task in bold. <b>Regex-based, checked on a sample of 60 tool calls: 57 of 60 (95%) labelled correctly</b> (30 of 30 calls outside the table rows were right; the 3 errors all add to the probe rows: a memory-folder read and two <code>python -c "import …"</code> checks counted as probes). Two earlier samples of 60 were used to fix the patterns before this one was drawn.</p>
